# Release signing — copy to `keystore.properties` and fill in.
#
# `keystore.properties` and `*.jks` are both gitignored. This example file is the
# only one that is committed, and it must never contain a real value.
#
# This project has no API keys, no database URLs and no third-party credentials —
# the app has no internet permission and talks to no service. The signing key is
# the only secret it has, and it is the one that matters: whoever holds it can
# publish an update that Android will install over a user's existing vault.
#
# Keep the .jks and this password somewhere you will still have in five years.
# Losing them does not just mean re-signing; it means every existing install can
# never be updated again, because Android refuses an APK signed by a different key.

storeFile=truevault-release.jks
storePassword=CHANGE_ME
keyAlias=CHANGE_ME
keyPassword=CHANGE_ME
